y  found that patients who had consumed a  high fiber diet  were about five times more likely to  respond preferably  to the anti-pd-1 treatment compared to  those on a low fiber diet  indicating that fiber is a significant  contributor  to response to cancer therapy so  this was part of the rationale in  designing this study to look at the  interaction between  diet and microbiome and how it  influences cancer therapy response and  toxicity  one of the main concerns that we see  with  with colon cancer patients is this  huge number of people dealing with  chemotherapy-induced diarrhea  it's really problematic especially  people undergoing full fox treatment  and up to five percent of those actually  die from um this chemotherapy  induced diarrhea and unfortunately  there's no clinical biomarkers  for it um the current strategies are  are insufficient um at this point and  it also has all the hallmarks of mucosal  damage and inflammation indicating that  there's a microbial component to this  and in fact  there was a study done several years ago  showing that there was a  significant change in the microbiome  over time as somebody was exposed to  radiotherapy and  the microbiome in association with  diarrhea so this prompted me  to put the study together to address  whether or not the diet or the  microbiome or the combination thereof  was actually predictive of chemotherapy  induced diarrhea and hopefully maybe we  can use that information to do something  to treat these people or even to prevent  cid  either through the diet or changes in  the microbiome and so  we're going to be recruiting through dod  funding recently  this study to recruit people with stage  two or three colon cancer about 120  individuals we're going to start by  setting up a sterile pathology suite to  collect those tissues  and blood and fecal samples as well as  food frequency questionnaires  at baseline and then before treatment  we'll get another  dietary information stool in blood and  then multiple times throughout  chemotherapy  we're going to be collecting stole blood  and dietary information  doing five um follow-up up to five years  and so hopefully  we'll be able to provide some maybe  biomarkers or information  for diet to prevent or reduce this  burden of chemotherapy induced diarrhea  in colon cancer patients  so the ultimate goal of this research i  hope in the future is actually to  identify  what are these tipping points and the  microbiome  that promote carcinogenesis if we  understand what those tipping points  are and find them before carcinogenesis  gets hold in that tissue then  perhaps we can prevent a carcinogen  that's occurring  overall or improve prognosis  and treatment response and so  understanding what these tipping points  are is a key  component of my lab so i just like to  acknowledge all of the folks in  my lab my two doctoral students julian  dill and my new doctoral student my  career who i didn't  have a chance to put on here yet um and  then several of my undergraduates who  are listed there the tabby lab has been  really instrumental in a lot of our in  vitro studies with outer membrane  vesicles  uh garth ehrlich's lab instrumental  again with the bacteria's fragile  studies and then my previous lab who i  still collaborate with extensively  and kurt harris and looking at  following up on those lung cancer  microbiome studies my biostatistician at  mayo clinic  june chen james white my  bioinformatician  and of course my funding resources there  if you want to contact me i'm at cancer  microbes especially  um if you are looking for a postdoc  position when you're hiring a post-doc  if you have skills in bioinformatics  microbiology or microbiome analysis  please get in touch with me  my twitter handle is down there as well  as my email  so i am happy to take any questions  thank you so much dr greyhouse for that  informative presentation  and we will now start the q a portion of  the webinar  if you have any questions you want to  ask please do so now just click  on that ask a question box located on  the far  left of your screen so let's get started  it looks like we already have some great  questions coming in from our audience  dr greyhouse are there any foods which  may  increase the risk of especially lung  cancer  well i would say that that's a really  great question  most of these foods which increase  cancer risk in general  have been mainly focused on inflammatory  related cancers  lung cancer is one of them but mainly  colon cancer  so without saying specific foods to  reduce lung cancer i would say foods  that are  anti-inflammatory that generally reduce  your inflammatory profile so eating  lots of fruits and vegetables and whole  grains in fact i always just  have giving this a lecture and my  nutrition class  anything that reduces your overall  inflammatory  milieu in your system so making sure you  get plenty of physical activity so  you're not  obese making sure really it's just  eating  a lot of colorful fruits and vegetables  with whole grains as the majority of  your diet i like the 80 20 rule for  myself  it works out well 80 of the time i eat  as healthy as i can  whole grains you don't have to eat  organic you don't need to do that but  you know whole grains whole fruits and  vegetables cooking for yourself  and 20 of the time you can eat whatever  you want and usually that  gets you to a point where you have a  good balance of  pro versus anti-inflammatory mildew in  your body  thank you for that our next question is  what sort of effect  does one's epigenetic have on microbiome  composition  and chances for lung cancer yeah that's  a really great question i think  that's some of the things that we really  don't understand yet  of and that's a question that i have  myself is we just don't  understand very well how  does the microbiome either change or  interact with  your epigenome we do know that butyrates  and  the short chain fatty acid can act at 